A||||5||Raydar||"Eating" ignition modules.||||5||
Z||000000||Raydar||01-28-2001||01:29 PM||||I've read many "igniton module" posts, but have yet to see this problem...<BR>One of the guys in our club has a car (86 SE with an Artero rebody) that is going through ignition modules at the rate of one every 300 miles.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>He's tried several from the Fiero Store, and several from GM. He is using the heat-sink compound. He said that the base-plate of the distributor looks like they have been "arcing" (burned spots, etc.) <BR>This happens using an aftermarket coil -Accel, I think, as well as a brand-new GM coil.<BR>He mentioned that he is using a replacement distributor that had a different part number than the original.<BR>Is it possible that he is getting the wrong modules for the distributor? Can anything be drawing excessive current and "taking out" the modules?<BR>BTW. He said his rear cooling fans are working properly. <BR>Any help will be appreciated.<P>Craig's Artero...<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Craig1_1.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 01-28-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||01-28-2001||03:05 PM||||Eating them at that rate could be a couple things. Yes, it's posible the distributor is wrong or some such thing. If there were wiring changes to acomodate the new dist then those changes need to be verified as correct.<P>First I'd make sure there are no ignition problems beyond the module eating. If the ignition is arching to ground anyplace it could fry the modules. <P>Second, read the heat managent article I just added to my cave. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A> this article is not anywhere near finished but it should give you a start.<P>Heat alone can kill the modules. In a kit car it's very posible there is a heat managment problem. Kits are built for looks first and anything else is an after thought.<P>You are dealing with both IR and convection. There will be no easy answers if it's a heat problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||01-28-2001||04:53 PM||||are you coating the bottom of module with dialectric compound before you screw it to baseplate? if not the heat will fry it quick.<P><BR>also check the little plastic wire/to module connector. they get real brittle and break allowing the two contacts to touch sometimes.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 01-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||01-28-2001||08:08 PM||||This is purely a guess...<P>Check the rotor - compare the distance from rotor tip to cap tower terminals, with that on a known good Fiero distributor (e.g. - <B>your</B> Fiero ?).  If your buddy's has to jump an unusually large distance, maybe the spark decides to hop down to the pickup coil, instead?  Or maybe the rotor has carbon track(s) that bleed juice down to the pickup coil?  Replacing the rotor shouldn't break the bank, in any case - might give it a try. <P>Another dumb thing to try (mostly for my sake, to humor me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) - attach a grounding clip from the distributor body, to a good engine ground and see if that helps...  ||||reg||5
Z||000004||Raydar||01-28-2001||08:21 PM||||Thanks, all. <BR>I e-mailed the link to this thread to Craig. Hopefully he'll find a fix here, and decide to join the Forum.<P>Just a bit of a tangent, but I was thinking about the rotor thing... <BR>I have seen a GM HEI rotor dead shorted. The particular one I dealt with formed a carbon track <I>through</I> the plastic, from the metal contact down to the distributor shaft.<BR>There was absolutely no spark at the plug. <BR>I've heard of other HEI rotors doing the same thing.<P>Please keep your ideas coming!<P>Thanks!<BR>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 01-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||lou_dias||01-29-2001||07:46 AM||||Yeah, the dielectric grease is critical!  I just had my ICM go Saturday and when I pulled off the old one, it wasn't spread out completely over the metal plate.  Also, make sure the ICM mounts completely flat against the distibutor so that the entire metal plate is making contact with the distributor in order to transfer heat properly.  Heat may have warped this area over time.||||reg||1
